AGENTS.md
.vscode
/uploads

# Database dumps.
#
# tmp/shop/backups/pre_shop_realign_2026-10-06.sql is tracked deliberately. It is
# the Phase 0 restore baseline referenced by tmp/shop/remedy.md, and it was
# checked rather than assumed: it holds the shop schema plus reference rows
# (products, reason codes) and empty order tables. No users table, no
# ewallet_ledger, no addresses or phone numbers.
#
# NEW dumps must not be committed. A mysqldump of a running environment carries
# every member row - password hashes, wallet balances, delivery addresses - and
# it lives in every clone and every repo backup long after those rows are gone
# from the live database. The tracked file above is the reason this rule exists:
# taking a dump "just to be safe" is a one-line action, not a decision.
tmp/shop/backups/*
!tmp/shop/backups/pre_shop_realign_2026-10-06.sql
*.sql.gz
/tmp/*.sql

# Credentials must never be committed, whatever the file extension. The QA
# document at tmp/shop/qa_test_v2.md carried a live admin password in plain text
# from commit 0e8efa9 until it was removed on 2026-10-10. Removing the line is
# not enough on its own: the string is still in history, so that password has
# to be rotated too. .gitignore cannot reach history, and this comment is the
# reminder.
#
# The patterns stay narrow on purpose - they want a credential-shaped filename.
# Something broad like *password* would hide legitimate documentation about
# authentication.
*.my.cnf
my.cnf
.env
.env.*

# One committed secret is knowingly still in the tree: the $cronKey default in
# midnight_reset.php. It is NOT fixed, only accepted - see ACCEPTED_ISSUES in
# tests/predeploy.php, which prints it on every deploy rather than hiding it.
# There is no ignore rule that can help here, because the secret is a literal
# inside a tracked source file.
